Chapter: ScopeLetArmEscape
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 BACKLOG 2.22, the adversarial half. `inner` is bound only inside the
 if-arm's let, and the arm has ended by the time `w + inner` runs, so
 the read is out of scope and this program must not compile.

 This rejects at the read, by name, with a line and a column. Getting
 there took removing the same aliasing bug from three places, and the
 diagnostic names which one is answering:

 The EMITTER used to answer, with `CDX2040: Unresolved call to 'inner'
 (redirected to __unresolved_trap)` -- a call the author never wrote --
 because `env-bind-local` and `scope-add-local` both wrote their binding
 into the CALLER's scope, so the name was still bound when the arm ended
 and both the resolver and the checker let it through.

 Then the CHECKER answered (CDX2002), once the type environment stopped
 aliasing. Now the RESOLVER answers (CDX3002), which is the right phase:
 an out-of-scope name is a resolution question, and the resolver reaches
 it first. If this test ever reports CDX2002 or CDX2040 again, a scope
 fork has been lost -- the program is still rejected, so only the CODE
 tells you the discipline regressed.

Section: Entry

  escapes : Integer -> Integer
  escapes (n) =
   let w = if n > 0 then let inner = n * 10 in inner + 1 else 0
   in w + inner

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni ("escapes: " & integer-to-text (escapes 2))
  end
